{
	title: 'Terminal tabs: tab bar and switching',
	description: `The terminal shows the host's tabs (task 0a) and one focused tab. A tab bar row above the prompt follows tasks/cc/terminal.md (Tabs): compact numeric labels, the focused one in brackets, one indicator character after a number when the tab is working, asks a question, failed or wants attention; with one tab it shows creation hints (ctrl-t: new), with several navigation hints (alt-#: goto, ctrl-n/p: switch); it stays one row, dropping hints from the lowest priority up and then the "Tabs:" label.
Keys: Ctrl-T opens a new tab in the focused tab's cwd, right after it; Ctrl-Shift-T reopens the most recently closed; Ctrl-W closes the focused tab (never the last); Ctrl-N and Ctrl-P go to the next and previous tab, wrapping; Alt-1..9 go to tab 1..9 and Alt-0 to tab 10.
Focus: a tab this client created or reopened gets focus; closing the focused tab goes back to the tab that opened it if that is still open and focus never left the new tab, else to its right neighbour (the new last tab if it was last); tabs opened, moved or closed by other clients never move this client's focus. The client follows the focused tab and tells the host it was seen. Each tab keeps its own client state while another is shown: editor state (cursor, selection, undo, history position; the text is the draft), editing the last prompt, completion choices; a modal closes on switch. A restart (Ctrl-R) comes back to the tab it left; a fresh ./run in a directory goes to that directory's tab (tab-start).
Test the focus rules as a pure function over (old list, new list, focused, what this client asked for), and the tab bar fitting at several widths.`,
	status: 'done',
	needs: ['0a', '1x', '3m'],
	notes: [
		'old-tabs.ts and old-session-tabs.ts (with its test) hold the old focus rules, including the opener return that expires as soon as focus leaves the new tab. old-tab-bar-excerpt.ts is the old tab bar: labels, indicators and hint dropping.',
		'Check keys.ts decodes Ctrl-Shift-T (kitty CSI 116;6u) apart from Ctrl-T; legacy terminals send the same byte for both, so there Ctrl-Shift-T cannot work. Alt-digit arrives as ESC digit in legacy mode.',
		'Switching tabs repaints the frame; the full-screen rules for that (peak padding, CSI 3J) are task vq. Until then a switch may use the existing force repaint.',
		'Decisions: the client follows only the focused tab (close the old, open the new on a switch) and ignores late events of other sessions; a tab shown again keeps its old transcript until the fresh snapshot replaces it (no mark where replayed history ends, task 66). The host publishes the tab list before the ack naming a tab, so focus moves on the ack (asked), not on the list. On every connection the client sends tab-start (cwd and last of the focused tab), which is also how it gets the list after a reconnect; open-newest is no longer used by the terminal.',
		'Restart: ./run exports HAL_TAB_FILE (mktemp, removed on exit); main.keepTab writes the focused tab id and cwd there on every change, main.lastTab reads it into app.state.start. The cwd matters: tab-start only honours last when it is in the given cwd.',
